Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 17:03:39 PST
From: raphael@fx.com (Glen Raphael)
Subject: Extropians Digest V93 #0022

Perry Metzger writes:

>> On the other hand, if I buy the S&P 500 as a basket, and
>> the index goes up $10, the person who sold me the basket has lost potential
>> income, but doesn't have to pay me anything -- the game is positive sum.

Timothy May writes:

>Why wouldn't the second case _also_ be a zero sum game? Maybe this is
>quibbling, but a "paper loss" is nearly as real to me as a "real" loss.

Suppose you read in the paper that the price of gold jumped 100% last week.
You've never owned any gold. Have you suffered a "paper loss"?  No.  Now,
suppose you owned gold at some point in the past, but sold it months ago.
Has the situation changed? No. Somebody else got richer from owning your
gold, and you didn't get richer but you didn't get poorer, either. In fact,
you might even be *better off* for having sold the gold. Maybe the reason you
sold your gold was to buy a stock that went up 200% last week and when you
saw the news about gold you said to yourself, "Boy, I'm sure glad I dumped
that gold when I did!"

Equity ownership, if the equities rise in value, is a positive sum game.

Now contrast that with futures contracts on equities. I think IBM's gonna
drop, so I pay Tim $10 for the right to buy an IBM share for $100 in three
month's time. I lose $10. Tim gains $10. If the price of IBM drops to $20
in three months, I exercise my option, whereupon I gain $80 and Tim loses
$80 (the difference between the current market value of the IBM share
and what I pay for it.)

Glen Raphael
raphael@fx.com

